1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
A few people ask me what you're actually meant to do as Scout when your team's on defence, or getting completely rolled,

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
or you're playing on some narrow map like Dust Bowl, where there's barely anywhere to move.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
And they all sound like different questions, but it's pretty much the same problem.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
You can't just hold W and take whatever fight you want anymore.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Because Scout feels ridiculous when your team's doing well.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Everyone's distracted, people are getting separated, you've got space to run around,

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
and you're usually the first person there whenever a fight opens up.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
But the second your team loses all that pressure, and every doorway starts getting watched,

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Scout can go from feeling like he has no limitations, to feeling like one of the most useless classes in the game.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
So a lot of this video is going to be about slowing down a bit.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Using chip damage, sitting further back on high ground where you can actually see what's going on,

12
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
and waiting for someone to overextend instead of launching yourself into 3 people every time you see their backs turned.

13
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Because even when an opening looks really good, you still have to think about whether your team is alive,

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
whether anyone can actually help you, and whether dying for that play is even worth it.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Especially when you're defending on red in Payload or Attack-Defend,

16
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
because you can't take your life for granted like Blue Team can.

17
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Because those respawn times on certain maps get just ridiculous.

18
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
And one bad death can take you out of the fight for what feels like forever.

19
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
And Scout can create way more for his team than the scoreboard actually shows.

20
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,000
You can make half the enemy team turn around, pull someone away from the objective, or force their medic backwards,

21
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
but your team still has to notice and do something with it.

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
So we're going to go through all of that with some pre-recorded clips that I'm going to lightly commentate,

23
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
and how to stay useful when you can't hold W.

24
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
How to know when to commit, and how to tell when you made a bad play,

25
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
versus when your team just didn't take advantage of what you created.

26
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
In downtime situations like this on defense, chip damage is sometimes all you can really do,

27
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
but it's more important than people think.

28
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
It makes people look back, slows them down, and forces them to look for heals.

29
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
And make sure not just when pestering people, but in general,

30
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
make sure you're on an elevated platform when holding any position with Scout.

31
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
It's crucial that if you get the chance, to not let Engineers get their sentry built in the first place.

32
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
If it's level 3, you alert your team, and when your combo comes out to deal with it,

33
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
help them if you can at that very moment, but otherwise pay attention to the flanks.

34
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The Engineer was on the ball here, and he starts building again,

35
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
so I call it out to my Soldier friend, and he deals with it straight away.

36
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
The Engineer's probably thinking, oh my god, I'm so unlucky,

37
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,000
they keep walking out as soon as I build my stuff, but it isn't luck,

38
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
I'm scouting out these areas, I'm being an actual Scout,

39
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
and I'm communicating what I see to my team.

40
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
At the same time, I'm not overextending, I'm treating these two flanks like they're my post.

41
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
I'm contesting them, and I'm cleaning up anyone who tries to walk through.

42
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
But I'm not running forward, trying to take a 1v2.

43
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
I'm just taking it slow, taking my time, and making sure these areas stay contested,

44
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,000
because after all, we're playing defence, we're not the ones attacking,

45
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
you don't need to hold W as much as you might think, just because you're playing Scout.

46
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Right here, I'm backed into a corner by a Pyro and a Scout.

47
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
It doesn't look like I have anywhere to go, but there's this tiny slope in the ground.

48
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
I run up it, jump backwards, and it gives me just enough height to double jump forward over the Pyro's head.

49
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
That tiny bit of elevation saves me from getting flamed,

50
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,000
and just happens to set me up to kill the Scout as well.

51
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
That's one of the best skills you can have as Scout, not just seamlessly using anything around you,

52
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
but identifying what can be used as high ground in the first place.

53
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
It's a bit like Emmett becoming a Master Builder at the end of the Lego Movie,

54
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
except you're looking at every box, railing, or tiny slope and thinking,

55
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
can I use that as high ground?

56
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Basically, any type of elevator platform will let you get extra height and reposition with less risk.

57
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Like the last clip, this is a defensive situation that isn't as straightforward as holding a boxed-in last with two obvious flanks.

58
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
When you're forced to fight in more open areas without much pressure from your team,

59
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Scout needs to take some of that pressure off them by becoming the centre of attention, without just throwing his life away.

60
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
It's about occupying spaces the enemy can't ignore, forcing them to deal with you,

61
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
and drawing attention away from your team while still prioritising survivability.

62
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Once your team is no longer occupying the space you were trying to defend,

63
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
and the enemy has taken full control of that area, your presence there becomes much less valuable.

64
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
If you still have space behind you to retreat into, use it.

65
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
There's no reason to keep testing an area your team no longer has any presence in, especially when you're on defence.

66
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Your job isn't to take the space back by yourself,

67
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
it's to stay alive, continue slowing them down, and keep running time off the clock.

68
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
But you need to be alive to do this.

69
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Where Blue Team is all about trying to get one big coordinated push to break through,

70
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Red is more about survivability.

71
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
The previous clip was a good example of what Scout should be doing when his team doesn't have much presence in the area,

72
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:58,000
but when your team is established and actually occupying the space you're defending, your role changes.

73
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Now your job is to prevent threats from reaching your team by pestering them,

74
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
or killing them like this Sniper I focus in on to prevent him landing an easy headshot on my Heavy or Med,

75
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
and clean up easy picks on enemies your Power Class is already engaging with,

76
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,000
and safely apply chip damage that forces Blue to retreat or delays their push.

77
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
You're still trying to have as much impact as possible,

78
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
but without over committing and throwing away the position your team has already established.

79
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Another question I got was how you're meant to play Scout on narrow maps like Dustbowl.

80
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Honestly, maps like Dustbowl and Gold Rush are where you need to take all of this advice the most seriously.

81
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:56,000
They're old, restrictive maps where the balances age pretty badly, especially for Red.

82
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Your respawn can be anywhere from 18 to 22 seconds, and the points can be captured absurdly quickly.

83
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
And because of that, there isn't some secret flank or defensive strategy

84
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
that suddenly makes you think that you're going to lose the game.

85
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
And because of that, there isn't some secret flank or defensive strategy

86
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
that suddenly makes Scout amazing.

87
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
You have to be extremely deliberate about where you position yourself,

88
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
which fights you take, and whether you're actually where your team needs you.

89
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
High ground is still important, but only if it's actually safe.

90
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Most of the obvious high ground on the first section of Dustbowl leaves you exposed to Snipers and Spam.

91
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Sometimes you genuinely have to play around the point,

92
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
and be prepared to back up once your team loses that space.

93
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
In this clip, I move around and I help my team defend the choke,

94
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
but I always end up returning to the same flank besides the point.

95
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
It gives me cover, close range fights, and an easy escape,

96
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,000
while still allowing me to directly defend the point.

97
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
It's also a really common place for Blue to put a teleporter,

98
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
so it's naturally somewhere Scout can find a lot of value.

99
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Target priority is also important.

100
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
In general, if you see something like a direct hit Soldier,

101
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,000
there's a good chance he's using it because he wants to destroy your team's sentry nest.

102
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Scout can choose his fights, so you should use that freedom to focus the people

103
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
who are most threatening to your team.

104
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
You need to play as a team better than Blue does on old maps like Gold Rush and Dustbowl.

105
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Something I like doing is briefly moving into Blue's territory,

106
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
and getting someone's attention, and then immediately retreating.

107
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Impatient players will often overreact and chase you too far,

108
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
which brings them directly into your team.

109
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,000
That's what happens to this Pyro when he follows me up the stairs just then,

110
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
and my Soldier friend already was holding that position behind me,

111
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
and I knew that, so he deals with the Pyro straight away.

112
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
I'm not just running away from the fight,

113
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,000
I'm dragging the Pyro into a fight that's much better for my team.

114
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Give Callouts a Scout, because I've already mentioned this,

115
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
but he's called Scout for a reason,

116
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
and you're often the first person who can see what Blue's doing,

117
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,000
so make your team aware of anything they deserve to know about.

118
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
It doesn't need to be complicated,

119
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
you know, it's just that their combo's coming through the top left flank,

120
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
or a Pyro's jetpacking towards you,

121
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
or someone is moving into your team's space.

122
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,000
Even basic information just gives your team time to react and capitalise.

123
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
Now I know not everyone wants to treat casual like a scrim,

124
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
and constantly give out Callouts,

125
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
but this Sixtus clip is the clearest way I can show you how powerful they are,

126
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and how powerful Scout becomes when his team can actually capitalise on the openings he creates.

127
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
I don't create this opening by dropping the Medic,

128
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
I create it by forcing basically their entire team to turn around and focus on me.

129
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,000
For those few seconds, none of them are looking at my team,

130
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,000
which gives everyone else the opportunity to move forward,

131
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
and wipe out basically their entire team.

132
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
But that's only powerful if my team knows what's happening,

133
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,000
I can't expect them to capitalise on the entire enemy team looking at me,

134
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:36,000
unless I call it out and make them aware.

135
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Once I do, they know that their opening exists,

136
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
and can immediately take advantage of it.

137
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
And this is what it looks like in full swing,

138
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
when your team capitalises on the opening Scout creates.

139
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
Your value isn't always the player you kill,

140
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
sometimes it's how much attention you pull away from everyone else,

141
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
and this absolutely applies to casual as well.

142
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
People just don't communicate it nearly enough,

143
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
especially as Scout.

144
